.TH MORIA 1 "2026-08-17" "moria 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
moria \- identify files and embedded firmware structures
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B moria
.RI [ options ]
.I <file|dir>
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B moria
maps what a file is and what known structures live inside it, with confidence
scores and sourced metadata. It is identification only: it locates and names
embedded formats (filesystems, containers, bootloaders, executables,
compression, media) with byte offsets and confidence tiers. It is
identification-first; with
.B \-\-extract
it also unpacks SquashFS, ext2/3/4, F2FS, JFFS2, UBIFS, romfs, YAFFS2, cramfs, FAT, exFAT, NTFS, HFS+, XFS, btrfs, EROFS, and ISO9660 filesystems, ZIP/tar/cpio archives, U-Boot uImage/FIT containers, and RAE Systems/Honeywell RFP firmware packages (LZARI-decompressing their sections)
internally (no sudo). Every other format is still identified and located by offset, ready for another tool such as binwalk.
.PP
Output is human-readable by default. A single file shows a findings tree
(containers nest their contents) followed by a run-stats footer; a directory
shows per-type counts and the notable findings grouped by directory, then the
same footer. The one-line assessment is carried in the JSON output only. Pass
.BR \-j / \-\-json
for a single JSON object, designed to be consumed by an LLM tool caller during
firmware and IoT assessments.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I <file|dir>
A file to identify, or a directory to scan recursively.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Output
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-json
Emit JSON instead of the human-readable view (the default). Use this when a
tool or model will parse the output.
.TP
.BR \-A ", " \-\-all
Keep interior compressed streams. By default moria drops \fBcompression\fR-category
findings (xz/gzip/lzo nodes) that fall inside a filesystem/container region \(em
the internal data nodes of a UBI/UBIFS or similar per-node flash filesystem,
which the filesystem extractor already recovers as real files. This applies to
both identification and \fB\-\-extract\fR (so extraction does not emit hundreds of
redundant single-node blobs). \fB\-A\fR restores them in both.
.TP
.BR \-E ", " \-\-entropy
Entropy analysis (off by default). Adds the \fBunidentified_regions\fR list \(em byte
ranges no finding claims, each with its Shannon entropy \(em and high-entropy
"possible encryption" hints in the assessment. The analysis is region-aware: a
filesystem's compressed interior (e.g. the xz data nodes inside a UBIFS volume)
is not reported as an unidentified high-entropy region, so the hint fires only on
genuinely unclaimed high-entropy data.
.TP
.B \-\-list
List tar/cpio/zip member names and sizes. Does not extract.
.SS Extract
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-extract
Unpack identified SquashFS, ext2/3/4, F2FS, JFFS2, UBIFS (incl. UBI), romfs, YAFFS2, cramfs, FAT, exFAT, NTFS, EROFS, and ISO9660 filesystems, ZIP/tar/cpio archives, U-Boot uImage/FIT containers, Android boot/sparse images, and RAE Systems/Honeywell RFP firmware packages into
.IR file .extracted/
(one subdir per region, named
.IR 0x<offset>\-<type> ).
Recurses into nested containers by default (a gzip-wrapped filesystem, a
filesystem embedded in another), re-scanning each produced file and unpacking
what it finds, bounded by
.BR \-\-depth ", " \-\-max\-files ", and " \-\-max\-bytes .
ext extraction is read-only and needs no sudo. Other formats are
identification-only. Takes a single file, not a directory.
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-outdir " " \fIdir\fR
Write extraction output to
.I dir
instead of the default
.IR file .extracted/ .
.TP
.BI \-\-depth " N"
Maximum extraction recursion depth (default 8).
.B \-\-depth 1
extracts one level only (no recursion).
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-files " N"
Stop extraction after
.I N
files have been produced across the whole run (default 500000); the manifest is
marked
.IR capped .
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-bytes " N"
Stop extraction after
.I N
output bytes (default 4294967296 = 4 GiB); the manifest is marked
.IR capped .
A per-extraction decompression-ratio guard (output/input over 1000:1 above
10 MiB) also stops descent into a likely decompression bomb.
.SS Carve
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-carve
Dump the raw byte range of each finding, and the unidentified gaps between
them, to
.IR file .carved/
as
.IR <offset> \- <type> .bin
files, WITHOUT parsing. This is the fallback when
.B \-\-extract
cannot reconstruct a filesystem (vendor obfuscation, an unsupported or corrupt
variant): the researcher still gets the raw bytes on disk to hand to another
tool or inspect by hand. A
.I manifest.json
records each blob's offset, size, and type. Independent of
.BR \-\-extract "; when both are given, carve writes to a sibling"
.IR file .carved/
directory. By default sub-kilobyte gaps (usually padding) are not carved;
.B \-A
carves every gap. Obeys
.B \-C
and
.BR \-\-max\-bytes .
Takes a single file, not a directory.
.SS Scan
.TP
.B \-\-broad
Also match the ~2500 generated general-format signatures (file(1) Magdir,
magic tier) on top of the firmware core set.
.SS Configuration
.TP
.BI \-\-sigs " dir"
Use a specific signatures directory instead of the bundled ones.
.TP
.BI \-\-threads " N"
Worker count for directory scans. Default: min(cores, 16).
.SS Information
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Runtime error (unreadable file, no signatures loaded).
.TP
.B 2
Usage error (unknown option, missing argument).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MORIA_SIGDIR
Overrides the signatures directory (same effect as \fB\-\-sigs\fR).
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
If set, \fBmoria\fR looks for signatures under \fB$XDG_DATA_HOME/moria\fR (else
\fB~/.local/share/moria\fR) as part of its search path.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disables bold section headers in \fB\-\-help\fR output.
.SH FILES
When no \fB\-\-sigs\fR or \fBMORIA_SIGDIR\fR is given, signatures are searched
for, in order, next to the binary (\fB<exe>/../signatures\fR and the install
sibling \fB<exe>/../share/moria/\fR), then \fB~/.local/share/moria\fR, then
\fB/usr/local/share/moria\fR and \fB/usr/share/moria\fR. \fBcmake \-\-install\fR
populates \fB<prefix>/share/moria/\fR.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Identify one file (human-readable):
.B moria firmware.bin
.TP
Identify one file as JSON (for a tool or model):
.B moria \-j firmware.bin
.TP
Unpack its filesystems:
.B moria \-e firmware.bin
.TP
Carve raw byte ranges to disk without parsing:
.B moria \-c firmware.bin
.TP
List archive contents without unpacking:
.B moria \-\-list update.tar
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR file (1),
.BR binwalk (1)
.PP
Full documentation: skills/moria/README.md
